### Description

I am trying to create a [`BlockingCollection<T>`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.blockingcollection-1) that rejects duplicate items. For this reason I wrote a custom collection that implements the [`IProducerConsumerCollection<T>`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.iproducerconsumercollection-1) interface, and then passed an instance of this collection to the constructor of the `BlockingCollection<T>`. 

The problem is that the bounding functionality of the `BlockingCollection<T>` seems to brake when a duplicate item has been rejected by the underlying collection. Although the item has been rejected, it still counts towards the [`BoundedCapacity`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.blockingcollection-1.boundedcapacity) limit. The result is that fewer than `BoundedCapacity` elements can be added in the collection, and eventually (after many rejected duplicates) the effective capacity is reduced to just 1.

### Reproduction Steps

```C#
using System;
using System.Collections;
using System.Collections.Concurrent;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        BlockingCollection<int> queue = new(new ConcurrentQueueNoDuplicates<int>(), 2);
        AddItem(1); // True
        AddItem(1); // InvalidOperationException
        AddItem(2); // False (unexpected)

        void AddItem(int value)
        {
            try
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"queue.TryAdd({value}): {queue.TryAdd(value)}");
            }
            catch (InvalidOperationException) when (!queue.IsAddingCompleted)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"queue.TryAdd({value}): InvalidOperationException");
            }
        }
    }

    private class ConcurrentQueueNoDuplicates<T> : IProducerConsumerCollection<T>
    {
        private readonly Queue<T> _queue = new();
        public bool TryAdd(T item)
        {
            lock (_queue)
            {
                if (_queue.Contains(item)) return false;
                _queue.Enqueue(item); return true;
            }
        }
        public bool TryTake(out T item)
        {
            lock (_queue)
            {
                if (_queue.Count == 0) { item = default; return false; }
                item = _queue.Dequeue(); return true;
            }
        }
        public int Count { get { lock (_queue) return _queue.Count; } }
        public bool IsSynchronized => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public object SyncRoot => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public void CopyTo(T[] array, int index) => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public void CopyTo(Array array, int index) => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public IEnumerator<T> GetEnumerator() => throw new NotImplementedException();
        IEnumerator IEnumerable.GetEnumerator() => throw new NotImplementedException();
        public T[] ToArray() => throw new NotImplementedException();
    }
}
```

Output:

```none
queue.TryAdd(1): True
queue.TryAdd(1): InvalidOperationException
queue.TryAdd(2): False
```

[Live demo](https://dotnetfiddle.net/BrE4h8).

### Expected behavior

The `BoundedCapacity` is configured with the value `2`, so it is expected that the two values 1 and 2 can both be stored in the collection. The attempt to add the value 1 twice should be denied, but the state of the collection should remain unchanged. So the subsequent `queue.TryAdd(2)` should return `true`.

### Actual behavior

The `queue.TryAdd(2)` returns `false`.

### Regression?

The actual behavior on .NET Framework 4.8 is the same.

### Known Workarounds

The underlying collection rejects an item by throwing an exception instead of returning `false`.

### Configuration

- .NET 6.0
- Windows 10
- 64-bit operating system, x64-based processor

### Other information

The [source code](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/56e821243d92f20a6abf7ca61288221f4d164dd1/src/libraries/System.Collections.Concurrent/src/System/Collections/Concurrent/BlockingCollection.cs#L480-L483) includes the following comment:

```C#
//TryAdd is guaranteed to find a place to add the element. Its return value depends
//on the semantics of the underlying store. Some underlying stores will not add an already
//existing item and thus TryAdd returns false indicating that the size of the underlying
//store did not increase.
```

The actual behavior of the `BlockingCollection<int>.TryAdd` is not to return `false`, but to throw an exception. The actual behavior is correctly [documented](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.blockingcollection-1.tryadd) in the online docs. My guess is that the specification changed during the development of the class, and the problem is somehow related to that change.

