> I'm not following: you're throwing an exception from your IProducerConsumerCollection.TryAdd, but that exception is being eaten by BlockingCollection and it's returning false from its TryAdd?

@stephentoub sorry, in my example I had given to an auxiliary method the confusing name `TryAdd`. I just renamed that method to `AddItem`.

The workaround that I've found is to change the `ConcurrentQueueNoDuplicates<T>.TryAdd` method like this:

```diff
public bool TryAdd(T item)
{
    lock (_queue)
    {
-       if (_queue.Contains(item)) return false;
+       if (_queue.Contains(item)) throw new Exception("The item already exists.");
        _queue.Enqueue(item); return true;
    }
}
```

After making this change in the underlying collection, the bounding functionality of the `BlockingCollection<T>` seems to continue working correctly after an attempt to insert a duplicate value with the [`TryAdd`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.blockingcollection-1.tryadd) method. With both versions the attempt throws. With the original version it throws an `InvalidOperationException`, and with the workaround it propagates the exception that is thrown by the underlying collection.

According to [the documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.blockingcollection-1.tryadd) an `InvalidOperationException` signifies that *"The underlying collection didn't accept the item."* My understanding is that handling (suppressing) this exception should be safe, I.e. it should leave the `BlockingCollection<T>` in a usable (unaltered) state. Its current behavior seems like a bug IMHO.

> According to [the documentation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.collections.concurrent.blockingcollection-1.tryadd) an InvalidOperationException signifies that "The underlying collection didn't accept the item." My understanding is that handling (suppressing) this exception should be safe, I.e. it should leave the BlockingCollection<T> in a usable (unaltered) state. Its current behavior seems like a bug IMHO.

My understanding of [the issue](https://dotnetfiddle.net/BrE4h8) is that `BlockingCollection<T>` is failing to roll back state in cases where the underlying IProducerConsumerCollection.TryAdd call returns false. If that's accurate it does sound like bug we might want to consider fixing.

@theodorzoulias would you be willing to contribute a potential fix?
